<description>
**Problem:** Signature-cache hashing relies on input requirements that are implicit at its public entry methods.
Inspired by the Elements rangeproof-cache vulnerability behind the recent [Liquid incident](https://blog.blockstream.com/liquid-network-security-incident-assessment) and the [subsequent cache-key fix](https://github.com/ElementsProject/elements/pull/1600), this change clarifies an easy-to-misread hashing pattern: `Write(data, size)` hashes the data bytes without encoding the size.
Bitcoin Core's cache keys already include the signature hash, public key, and signature in separate ECDSA and Schnorr domains.
Bitcoin Core's signature-cache encoding is unambiguous for inputs accepted by its current script checker: a valid ECDSA public key's prefix determines its length, while a Schnorr public key is always 32 bytes, leaving the signature as the remaining suffix.

**Fix:** Use native `HashWriter` serialization in the typed `ComputeEntry*` methods so ECDSA public keys and signature vectors include their CompactSize lengths.
Schnorr retains its fixed-size public-key encoding and raw signature span.
Return the digest by value from the public `ComputeEntry*` methods so callers explicitly initialize the cache entry, where the previous chained `Finalize(entry.begin())` obscured the output assignment.
Keep the prepared salt prefixes private and const, and copy them locally for each entry.
Retain the ECDSA salt padding and drop the Schnorr salt padding.
Add a nonce-taking constructor so tests can reproduce the encoding deterministically.
The process-local cache-entry bytes change, while script validity rules are unchanged.
</description>

<commits>
ba5ef05fb9 test: precompute script transaction data

Precompute transaction data for the existing script vectors so plain and caching verification can share the same immutable transactions.

5b9cc7a490 test: compare plain and cached verification

Require caching to preserve the existing script vectors' results and errors and the fuzz inputs' verification results.
Repeat script verification after successful signatures are stored, and alternate plain and caching checkers under random flag combinations.
Allow empty ECDSA signatures, which can remain after removing the sighash byte.
Repeat fuzz verification to detect caching of failed checks.

9378db6f0b test: cover public-key encoding lengths

Extend `pubkey_unserialize` to check every header and lengths through one byte beyond the largest key.
Require construction and deserialization to agree, and round-trip each resulting key without unread bytes.

d0204b07d0 test: cover malformed ECDSA public keys

Extend an existing lax-encoding vector with a non-empty, wrong-length public key to exercise rejection by the script checker.

dcf5ad544f test: cover ECDSA signatures padded past 255 bytes

Lax DER verification accepts trailing data, including signatures longer than 255 bytes.
Add a padded P2PK vector to exercise that accepted input through both plain and caching verification.

fbfca28a41 refactor: return signature-cache entries by value

Return entries through the public `ComputeEntry*` methods so verification sites explicitly initialize them.
This replaces output parameters whose assignment was hidden in the chained `Finalize(entry.begin())`.
The hashing procedure and serialized bytes are unchanged.

8696231914 test: cover Schnorr cache inputs

Extend the BIP341 key-path vector test to check insertion, non-storing hits, entries that distinguish public keys and sighashes, and rejection without caching of a correctly sized invalid signature.
Cover both 64- and 65-byte script signatures and the exact errors for malformed sizes and an explicit default sighash byte.
Use `uint32_t` for the checker index to avoid narrowing in brace initialization, and require successful signing before taking a 64-byte signature span.

79d82f745f script: prepare immutable signature-cache prefixes

Make both prepared hash prefixes const `HashWriter` members and copy them inside `ComputeEntryECDSA` and `ComputeEntrySchnorr` before hashing an entry.
Serialize the nonce followed by a one-byte signature-type tag.
Keep 31 zero bytes after the ECDSA tag so the first SHA256 block is compressed once during construction.
With it, 70-72-byte ECDSA signatures need three compressions per entry for both compressed and uncompressed keys, while unpadded uncompressed keys would need four.
Drop the Schnorr padding, since Schnorr still needs three compressions and was slightly faster in local ARM SHA256 measurements.
The cache is process-local, so the changed entry bytes have no persisted compatibility effect.
Add a nonce-taking constructor so encoding tests do not depend on global RNG sequencing.
Characterize the raw field encoding in these public methods before the following native-serialization change.

Suggested by Yudis-bit: https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/36395#discussion_r4499982215

Co-authored-by: Yudis-bit <Yudis-bit@proton.me>

a7c23f4278 script: serialize signature-cache inputs

Use HashWriter's native serialization for the public key and signature, following the Elements proof-cache change (https://github.com/ElementsProject/elements/pull/1600).
ECDSA public keys and signature vectors include their CompactSize lengths, while Schnorr retains its fixed-size public-key encoding and raw signature span.
The cache no longer relies on the ECDSA key's header byte to delimit the signature.
</commits>

